<!--
    An argument carried across a call and returned in its own register is a
    return value, not leftover.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 call and prototype model, output-prototype.
    P0 assertion:      option retinputhalf on|off  (default on).

    Source (clang -O2 -target arm-linux-gnueabi, bytes verbatim below with each
    `bl ext` pointed at 0x100100, where `ext` has no body):

        void ext(void);
        unsigned long long own(unsigned a, unsigned b)
            { ext(); return (unsigned long long)b << 32 | a; }
        unsigned long long own_cond(unsigned a, unsigned b, int n)
            { if (n) ext(); return (unsigned long long)b << 32 | a; }
        int keep_int(int a, int b) { ext(); return a; }

        own:      push {r4,r5,r11,lr}; mov r4,r1; mov r5,r0; bl ext
                  mov r0,r5; mov r1,r4; pop {r4,r5,r11,lr}; bx lr
        keep_int: push {r4,lr}; mov r4,r0; bl ext; mov r0,r4; pop {r4,lr}; bx lr

    and, hand-written, the same value returned on two paths:

        two_ret:  cmp r2,#0; bxeq lr
                  push {r4,r5,r11,lr}; mov r4,r1; mov r5,r0; bl ext
                  mov r0,r5; mov r1,r4; pop {r4,r5,r11,lr}; bx lr

    `own` keeps b in r4 across the call and moves it back into r1, its own
    argument register, to return it as the high word. After copy propagation
    r1 reaches the RETURN as the caller's own r1, which is also what an
    untouched register looks like, so the pair repair dropped it: pass 2
    (option retinputhalf off, the strict rule) shows the old output,
    `unsigned int own(unsigned int a0)`, with b gone from the prototype. Return
    recovery still sees the two moves and records r1 as moved back, so by
    default the pair and the parameter stay. `own_cond` moves the values on
    both paths of the branch. `two_ret` leaves r0 and r1 untouched on its early
    return; the other return proves the function returns the pair, so both
    returns keep it. `keep_int` is the control: r1 is killed by the call,
    nothing moves it back, and it stays a one-register int function.
-->
